-- title:
--   The reduced traces of μΛ form qq'ℤ
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ and $q'$ be primes with $q' \neq q$, and let $a, b \in \mathbb{Q}$ be such that the quaternion algebra $B = \mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q}, a, b]$ satisfies `IsIndefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b q q'`, i.e. $0 < a$ or $0 < b$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of the ring of integers of $\mathbb{Q}$ the base change of $B$ to the $v$-adic completion of $\mathbb{Q}$ has all its nonzero elements invertible precisely when $v$ contains $q$ or contains $q'$. Let $\Lambda$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of $B$ which is a maximal order, meaning that $\Lambda$ contains $1$, is closed under multiplication, spans $B$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and is finitely generated, and that every order containing $\Lambda$ equals $\Lambda$. Let $\mu \in \Lambda$ satisfy $\mu^2 = -(qq') \cdot 1$ in $B$. The conclusion is twofold: first, for every $z \in \Lambda$ there is an integer $t$ with $\operatorname{trd}(\mu z) = qq' \, t$, where $\operatorname{trd}(x) = 2x_{\mathrm{re}}$; second, there exists $z \in \Lambda$ with $\operatorname{trd}(\mu z) = qq'$ exactly. Together these say that $\operatorname{trd}(\mu \Lambda) = qq'\mathbb{Z}$.
--
--   The element $\mu$ generates the different of the maximal order $\Lambda$ in an indefinite quaternion algebra of discriminant $qq'$, and the statement identifies the image of the different under the reduced trace as the ideal generated by the discriminant. It is used in the construction of the trace pairing on $\Lambda$ attached to $\mu$, and in particular in the verification that the alternating form $z \mapsto \operatorname{trd}(\mu z)/qq'$ is a perfect pairing.
